<problem><title>Video review questions</title>
<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />

<p>The graph of <m>\frac{dz}{dt} = f(t)</m> is shown below. Which choice best describes the behavior of the solution, <m>z(t)</m>, as <m>t</m> increases?</p>

<graph grid="true" xmin="-1" xmax="8" ymin="-2" ymax="4">
  <function name="f">-(1/4)(x-1)(x-7)
    <label><m>\frac{dz}{dt}</m></label>
  </function>
</graph>
<answer name="ans1a">
  <choice><m>z(t)</m> increases until it reaches approximately <m>t = 4</m>, then it decreases</choice>
  <choice credit="1"><m>z(t)</m> starts off decreasing, changes direction and increases at <m>t = 1</m>, then begins to decrease again at <m>t = 7</m></choice>
  <choice><m>z(t)</m> increases continuously as <m>t</m> increases</choice>
</answer>

</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>The graph of <m>z(t)</m> from the previous question is shown below along with the graph of its derivative. You can adjust the initial condition for <m>z(t)</m> with the slider below the graph.</p>

<graph grid="true" xmin="-4" xmax="10" ymin="-6" ymax="12">
  $f
  <function name="sol" styleNumber="2">-(x^3)/12 + x^2 -(7/4)x +$C
    <label><m>z(t)</m></label>
  </function>
</graph>
<slider name="C" from="-4" to="4">
  <label>Adjust initial condition, <m>C</m></label>
</slider>

<p>What effect does changing the initial condition have on the behavior of the solution <m>z(t)</m>?</p>
<answer name="ans1b">
  <choice credit="1">changing the initial condition shifts the graph of the solution vertically</choice>
  <choice>changing the initial condition shifts the graph of the solution horizontally</choice>
  <choice>changing the initial condition stretches the graph of the solution vertically</choice>
</answer>
</exercise>
</problem>

</section>